# Inline extensions {#inline}

Underscore emphasis applies at word boundaries: _em_ and __strong__ work
just like *em* and **strong**.

Intraword underscores stay literal because the run is flanked by
alphanumerics on both sides. Examples: snake_case identifiers, my_var_name
references, and snake__case patterns are preserved as plain text.

Code spans support runs of one, two, or three backticks: `single`, ``two ` ticks``,
and ```three backticks `` two `` runs``` inside.

Backslash escapes the extended set: \_underscore\_, \!bang, \.dot, \-dash,
\+plus, \<less, \>greater, \\backslash, \"quote\", \'apos\'.

Hard line break with backslash: first half\
second half on the next line.
